##DESCRIPTION
##  Algebra problem: true or false for inequality 
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('algebra', 'inequality', 'fraction')

## DBsubject('Algebra')
## DBchapter('Fundamentals')
## DBsection('Real Numbers')
## Date('6/3/2002')
## Author('')
## Institution('')
## TitleText1('Precalculus')
## EditionText1('3')
## AuthorText1('Stewart, Redlin, Watson')
## Section1('1.1')
## Problem1('22')

########################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
"PGstandard.pl",
"MathObjects.pl",
"parserRadioButtons.pl",
);

TEXT(beginproblem());
##############################################################
#
#  Setup
Context("Numeric");

$radio1 = RadioButtons(
  ["\(\Omega = [-100, 100]^4\)","other wrong answers","None of these"],
  "\(\Omega = [-100, 100]^4\)", # correct answer
  last => ["None of these"], # can be a list
$radio2 = RadioButtons(
  ["wrong answers","\( A = \{\)(\(a,b,c,d\)) \(\in \mathbb{R}^4 | ad = bc\}\)","None of these"],
  ""\( A = \{\lbrace a,b,c,d \rbrace \in \mathbb{R}^4 | ad = bc}\)", # correct answer
  last => ["None of these"], # can be a list
  
$radio3 = RadioButtons(
  ["0", "other wrong answers","None of these"],
  "0", # correct answer
  last => ["None of these"], # can be a list

$radio4 = RadioButtons(
  ["You must first verify that \(\Omega \in \mathbb{R}^n\) is a Jordan measurable set, that \(\\sum\) is all Jordan measurable subsets of \(\Omega\) and that \(\phi : \Omega \to [0,\infty)\) is a bounded non-negative function that is continuous on \(\Omega\) except for a set of zero Jordan measure.","other wrong answers","None of these"],
  ""You must first verify that \(\Omega \in \mathbb{R}^n\) is a Jordan measurable set, that \(\\sum\) is all Jordan measurable subsets of \(\Omega\) and that \(\phi : \Omega \to [0,\infty)\) is a bounded non-negative function that is continuous on \(\Omega\) except for a set of zero Jordan measure.", # correct answer
  last => ["None of these"], # can be a list
  
);
##############################################################
#
#  Text
#
#

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
Random matrices possess a beautiful interplay between linear algebra, calculus, and probability. They also have remarkable applications across many scientific areas. Here you will study one such question $BR

The set of 2×2 matrices with all entries in [−100, 100] can be viewed as a subset of \(\mathbb{R}^4\). Choose a matrix M from this subset of \(\mathbb{R}^4\) with a uniform probability function. What is the probability that the matrix M is not invertible?

$BR
$BR
Precisely define the probability space (\(\Omega, \sum, \mathbb{P}\)) needed to answer this question.
\{ $radio1->buttons() \}

$BR
$BR

Express the invertibility question in terms of \(\mathbb{P}\)(A) for some well chosen event A.

\{ $radio2->buttons() \}

$BR
$BR

What do you conjecture is the value of \(\mathbb{P}\)(A)?

\{ $radio3->buttons() \}

$BR
$BR

Before you prove your conjecture about P(A), what do you first need to verify?
\{ $radio4->buttons() \}

$BR
$BR

END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;
Context()->normalStrings;

##############################################################
#
#  Answers
#
#
install_problem_grader(~~&std_problem_grader);

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ANS( $radio1->cmp() );
ANS( $radio2->cmp() );
ANS( $radio3->cmp() );
ANS( $radio4->cmp() );

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_SOLUTION
${PAR}SOLUTION:$PAR
The correct answer is \{ $radio1->correct_ans() \}
The correct answer is \{ $radio2->correct_ans() \}
The correct answer is \{ $radio3->correct_ans() \}
The correct answer is \{ $radio4->correct_ans() \}
END_SOLUTION
Context()->normalStrings;

COMMENT('MathObject version.');

ENDDOCUMENT();      
